fix(training): the gateway must bind its port, and job claiming is opt-in (#218)

`node training/dist/index.js` — the container CMD — logged "Gateway starting..." and exited 0
without binding a socket, while docker-compose mapped 8001, the Dockerfile declared EXPOSE 8001, and
the reference showed curl examples against localhost:8001. Under `restart: unless-stopped` the
service crash-looped into a state `saip status` reads as running. Every Learning API route was
reachable only inside a test process, which is how 97 passing training tests coexisted with a service
that served nothing.

Adds `TrainingServerService` (health endpoint + mounted learning-api app + `start()`), resolves the
listen port as SAIP_TRAINING_PORT → saip.yaml `training.port` → 8001, and drives it from the entry
point so the process stays up.

Job claiming is deliberately not automatic: `SAIP_TRAINING_RUN=1` opts in. On a single card training
and serving are time-shared through `saip awake`/`saip sleep`, so a gateway that claimed queued jobs
the moment it booted would take VRAM from whatever was serving — the operator would see an OOM in
whichever process lost rather than a queue that waited.

`tests/serve-program.test.ts` starts the built artifact, asserts a real HTTP response and that the
process is still alive. It fails against the previous entry point, which is the canary.

Part of #218
